# Build context exclusions.
#
# Two jobs. The first is speed: the context is tarred and sent to the daemon
# before any instruction runs, and node_modules alone is hundreds of megabytes
# that `npm ci` is about to reinstall for Linux anyway.
#
# The second is safety, and it is the one that matters. Anything reaching the
# context can be COPYed into a layer, and a layer that once held a secret still
# holds it after a later instruction deletes the file. A published image is
# public. So every pattern .gitignore uses for credentials is repeated here —
# .gitignore does not apply to Docker builds, and assuming it does is how keys
# ship.

# Dependencies and build output — reinstalled/rebuilt inside the image
node_modules/
**/node_modules/
server/dist/
client/dist/
coverage/
**/coverage/

# Secrets. Mirrors .gitignore; see the note above about why this is not
# redundant.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
server/.env
**/serviceAccountKey*.json
**/*firebase-adminsdk*.json
**/sprout-dev-*.json
deploy/*.env

# Version control and local tooling state
.git/
.gitignore
.github/
.vercel/
.worktrees/
.superpowers/
.graphify/
.claude/
.agents/

# Editor and OS noise
.DS_Store
*.swp
.vscode/
.idea/

# Emulator and debug artefacts
firebase-debug.log
firestore-debug.log
*.log

# Documentation and course material — none of it is needed at runtime, and it
# is a large share of the repository by file count.
md/
docs/
Sprout_Vault/
Raw dump/
transcripts/
**/*.md
!server/FIREBASE_SETUP.md

# Test fixtures the runtime image never reads
**/__tests__/
server/tests/
client/src/**/*.test.ts
client/src/**/*.test.tsx

# Docker's own files — changing compose must not invalidate the build cache
docker-compose*.yml
**/Dockerfile
.dockerignore
